1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,399
Two papers said this: a dispute about how somebody died goes to a private

2
00:00:04,399 --> 00:00:05,062
arbitrator.

3
00:00:05,362 --> 00:00:08,288
A dispute about an unpaid bill stays in a courthouse.

4
00:00:10,088 --> 00:00:15,237
The case is Marmet Health Care Center against Clayton Brown, decided by the Supreme

5
00:00:15,237 --> 00:00:18,215
Court of the United States on February 21, 2012.

6
00:00:18,515 --> 00:00:24,039
It is five pages long, and it is per curiam — an opinion issued by the Court with no

7
00:00:24,039 --> 00:00:26,735
author's name on it and no vote reported.

8
00:00:27,035 --> 00:00:28,057
Five pages.

9
00:00:28,357 --> 00:00:32,734
Almost everything a person would want to know about these three families is not in

10
00:00:32,734 --> 00:00:33,001
them.

11
00:00:34,801 --> 00:00:38,751
This litigation involves three negligence suits against nursing homes in West

12
00:00:38,751 --> 00:00:39,213
Virginia.

13
00:00:39,513 --> 00:00:42,253
That is the opinion's own first line of fact.

14
00:00:42,553 --> 00:00:46,686
The suits were brought by Clayton Brown, Jeffrey Taylor, and Sharon Marchio.

15
00:00:46,986 --> 00:00:49,169
Brown sued Marmet Health Care Center.

16
00:00:49,469 --> 00:00:50,444
So did Taylor.

17
00:00:50,744 --> 00:00:53,066
Marchio sued a nursing home in Clarksburg.

18
00:00:53,366 --> 00:00:57,906
In the Supreme Court's caption it has two names — Clarksburg Nursing Home and

19
00:00:57,906 --> 00:01:02,329
Rehabilitation Center, doing business as Clarksburg Continuous Care Center.

20
00:01:02,629 --> 00:01:06,251
She sued as executrix of the estate of Pauline Virginia Willett.

21
00:01:06,551 --> 00:01:07,758
Three plaintiffs.

22
00:01:08,058 --> 00:01:09,265
Two nursing homes.

23
00:01:09,565 --> 00:01:10,494
One state.

24
00:01:10,794 --> 00:01:13,952
The first order came on August 25, 2009.

25
00:01:14,252 --> 00:01:17,642
The Circuit Court of Kanawha County dismissed Clayton Brown's suit.

26
00:01:17,942 --> 00:01:23,348
Brown had argued that the arbitration clause was unenforceable on two grounds — that

27
00:01:23,348 --> 00:01:28,303
it violated section 15(c) of the state's Nursing Home Act, and that it was an

28
00:01:28,303 --> 00:01:30,620
unconscionable contract of adhesion.

29
00:01:30,920 --> 00:01:35,184
The circuit court disagreed, and ruled that under the admission agreement signed

30
00:01:35,184 --> 00:01:38,861
with the nursing home he was required to arbitrate all of his claims.

31
00:01:39,161 --> 00:01:41,483
That order is one paragraph long.

32
00:01:41,783 --> 00:01:44,569
One paragraph.

33
00:01:44,869 --> 00:01:49,609
Its stated basis is a single clause: the court determined that Brown was required to

34
00:01:49,609 --> 00:01:54,123
arbitrate his claims after hearing argument of counsel, reviewing the respective

35
00:01:54,123 --> 00:01:55,364
briefs and the record.

36
00:01:55,664 --> 00:01:56,686
No findings.

37
00:01:56,986 --> 00:01:58,054
No analysis.

38
00:01:58,354 --> 00:02:00,862
That paragraph comes back at the end of this story.

39
00:02:01,162 --> 00:02:04,134
Five weeks later, the same court did the same thing.

40
00:02:04,434 --> 00:02:09,799
The second order came on September 29, 2009, and it dismissed Jeffrey Taylor's suit

41
00:02:09,799 --> 00:02:14,001
against owners, operators and employees of the same nursing home.

42
00:02:14,301 --> 00:02:16,484
Taylor had made the same two arguments.

43
00:02:16,784 --> 00:02:21,019
The circuit court concluded that under the admission agreement he was required to

44
00:02:21,019 --> 00:02:24,261
arbitrate all of the claims asserted against the nursing home.

45
00:02:24,561 --> 00:02:26,279
Both plaintiffs appealed.

46
00:02:26,579 --> 00:02:28,390
The third order went the other way.

47
00:02:28,690 --> 00:02:33,563
On June 2, 2010, the Circuit Court of Harrison County refused to dismiss Sharon

48
00:02:33,563 --> 00:02:36,585
Marchio's suit and refused to compel arbitration.

49
00:02:36,885 --> 00:02:41,133
Instead it certified a question upward, because Marchio had argued that her clause

50
00:02:41,133 --> 00:02:44,501
was unenforceable under the same section of the Nursing Home Act.

51
00:02:44,801 --> 00:02:49,027
The question was whether that section was preempted by the Federal Arbitration Act.

52
00:02:49,327 --> 00:02:53,599
Two dismissals and one question, all travelling to the state's highest court.

53
00:02:53,899 --> 00:02:55,942
Now the part where the record stops.

54
00:02:56,242 --> 00:02:59,764
Here is everything the Supreme Court's opinion tells you about what happened to

55
00:02:59,764 --> 00:03:00,700
these three patients.

56
00:03:01,000 --> 00:03:02,533
It is two sentences.

57
00:03:02,833 --> 00:03:07,368
In each case, a family member of a patient requiring extensive nursing care had

58
00:03:07,368 --> 00:03:11,099
signed an agreement with a nursing home on behalf of the patient.

59
00:03:11,399 --> 00:03:15,896
In each of the three cases, a family member of a patient who had died sued the

60
00:03:15,896 --> 00:03:20,335
nursing home in state court, alleging that negligence caused injuries or harm

61
00:03:20,335 --> 00:03:21,430
resulting in death.

62
00:03:21,730 --> 00:03:23,170
That is the whole account.

63
00:03:23,470 --> 00:03:24,445
No ages.

64
00:03:24,745 --> 00:03:25,813
No conditions.

65
00:03:26,113 --> 00:03:28,481
No dates of admission, no dates of death.

66
00:03:28,781 --> 00:03:33,259
No description of the care, and no description of what the negligence was said to

67
00:03:33,259 --> 00:03:33,425
be.

68
00:03:33,725 --> 00:03:38,261
Pauline Virginia Willett is the only patient named anywhere in the opinion, and she

69
00:03:38,261 --> 00:03:40,830
is named only in the caption of Marchio's case.

70
00:03:41,130 --> 00:03:43,034
Brown's patient is unnamed.

71
00:03:43,334 --> 00:03:44,681
So is Taylor's.

72
00:03:44,981 --> 00:03:48,696
The signature is described twice, in the same four words.

73
00:03:48,996 --> 00:03:50,157
A family member.

74
00:03:50,457 --> 00:03:52,361
The opinion never says which.

75
00:03:52,661 --> 00:03:56,919
It never says whether the person who signed is the person who later sued, and it

76
00:03:56,919 --> 00:03:59,581
never says what either of them was to the patient.

77
00:03:59,881 --> 00:04:02,946
A five-page per curiam opinion is not a narrative.

78
00:04:03,246 --> 00:04:06,172
What the record does describe closely is the paper.

79
00:04:06,472 --> 00:04:10,692
The relevant parts of the agreements in Brown's case and Taylor's case were

80
00:04:10,692 --> 00:04:11,255
identical.

81
00:04:11,555 --> 00:04:16,237
Both contained a clause requiring the parties to arbitrate all disputes, other than

82
00:04:16,237 --> 00:04:19,171
claims to collect late payments owed by the patient.

83
00:04:19,471 --> 00:04:22,304
So which dispute did the paper leave in a courthouse?

84
00:04:22,604 --> 00:04:23,765
Not the death.

85
00:04:24,065 --> 00:04:26,991
The money.

86
00:04:27,291 --> 00:04:30,077
There was a second provision in those two contracts.

87
00:04:30,377 --> 00:04:34,647
The party filing the arbitration is responsible for paying a filing fee, in

88
00:04:34,647 --> 00:04:39,201
accordance with the Rules of the American Arbitration Association fee schedules.

89
00:04:39,501 --> 00:04:41,962
Whoever starts the proceeding pays to start it.

90
00:04:42,262 --> 00:04:44,677
The opinion states no dollar figure.

91
00:04:44,977 --> 00:04:47,020
Marchio's paper was not that paper.

92
00:04:47,320 --> 00:04:52,196
The agreement in Marchio's case also included a clause requiring arbitration, but it

93
00:04:52,196 --> 00:04:56,782
made no exceptions to the arbitration requirement and it did not mention filing

94
00:04:56,782 --> 00:04:57,072
fees.

95
00:04:57,372 --> 00:04:59,369
No carve-out for late payments.

96
00:04:59,669 --> 00:05:01,480
Nothing about who pays to file.

97
00:05:01,780 --> 00:05:03,034
Three families.

98
00:05:03,334 --> 00:05:04,402
Two documents.

99
00:05:04,702 --> 00:05:07,906
The state law they were all pointing at was one sentence.

100
00:05:08,206 --> 00:05:13,969
Section 15(c) of the West Virginia Nursing Home Act, as it stood in 1997: any waiver

101
00:05:13,969 --> 00:05:19,664
by a resident or his or her legal representative of the right to commence an action

102
00:05:19,664 --> 00:05:25,290
under this section, whether oral or in writing, shall be null and void as contrary

103
00:05:25,290 --> 00:05:26,457
to public policy.

104
00:05:26,757 --> 00:05:28,893
A waiver of the right to commence an action.

105
00:05:29,193 --> 00:05:30,493
Null and void.

106
00:05:30,793 --> 00:05:35,019
West Virginia's legislature had put that in the statute books in 1997.

107
00:05:35,319 --> 00:05:40,657
Whether it survived contact with a federal statute passed in 1925 was now a question

108
00:05:40,657 --> 00:05:42,564
for the state's highest court.

109
00:05:44,364 --> 00:05:49,343
On June 29, 2011, West Virginia's Supreme Court of Appeals decided all three cases

110
00:05:49,343 --> 00:05:49,890
together.

111
00:05:50,190 --> 00:05:54,791
It consolidated Brown's and Taylor's appeals with Marchio's, which was before the

112
00:05:54,791 --> 00:05:56,041
court on other issues.

113
00:05:56,341 --> 00:06:01,229
The court's own later description of what it produced: an extensive opinion with

114
00:06:01,229 --> 00:06:02,146
three holdings.

115
00:06:02,446 --> 00:06:04,118
Lawyers call it Brown One.

116
00:06:04,418 --> 00:06:06,601
The first holding went against the patients.

117
00:06:06,901 --> 00:06:11,181
The state court took its own legislature's waiver ban and held that the Federal

118
00:06:11,181 --> 00:06:12,752
Arbitration Act preempted it.

119
00:06:13,052 --> 00:06:15,978
The reasoning was a rule the court stated for itself.

120
00:06:16,278 --> 00:06:21,448
A state statute, rule, or common-law doctrine which targets arbitration provisions

121
00:06:21,448 --> 00:06:26,240
for disfavored treatment, and which is not usually applied to other types of

122
00:06:26,240 --> 00:06:31,347
contract provisions, stands as an obstacle to the accomplishment and execution of

123
00:06:31,347 --> 00:06:36,201
the purposes and objectives of the Federal Arbitration Act, and is preempted.

124
00:06:36,501 --> 00:06:38,080
That is the rule it wrote.

125
00:06:38,380 --> 00:06:40,377
Then it applied that rule to Charleston.

126
00:06:40,677 --> 00:06:45,966
To the extent that section 15(c) attempts to nullify and void any arbitration clause

127
00:06:45,966 --> 00:06:51,129
in a written contract which evidences a transaction affecting interstate commerce,

128
00:06:51,129 --> 00:06:55,789
between a nursing home and a nursing home resident or the resident's legal

129
00:06:55,789 --> 00:06:58,371
representative, the statute is preempted.

130
00:06:58,671 --> 00:07:03,083
The picture of a state court simply defying Washington does not survive that.

131
00:07:03,383 --> 00:07:06,494
On the statute, West Virginia agreed with the nursing homes.

132
00:07:06,794 --> 00:07:10,927
Nobody appealed that holding, and the court would later note that nobody had.

133
00:07:11,227 --> 00:07:13,688
The second holding is the one that travelled.

134
00:07:13,988 --> 00:07:18,650
It is Syllabus Point 21, and it reads: Congress did not intend for arbitration

135
00:07:18,650 --> 00:07:23,611
agreements, adopted prior to an occurrence of negligence that results in a personal

136
00:07:23,611 --> 00:07:28,214
injury or wrongful death, and which require questions about the negligence be

137
00:07:28,214 --> 00:07:32,518
submitted to arbitration, to be governed by the Federal Arbitration Act.

138
00:07:32,818 --> 00:07:34,490
The load is on one word.

139
00:07:34,790 --> 00:07:35,719
Prior.

140
00:07:36,019 --> 00:07:37,087
Prior to what?

141
00:07:37,387 --> 00:07:39,663
The court's argument was about sequence.

142
00:07:39,963 --> 00:07:44,189
A commercial contract that goes wrong produces a dispute about the contract.

143
00:07:44,489 --> 00:07:49,397
A nursing home admission agreement that goes wrong produces something else entirely

144
00:07:49,397 --> 00:07:54,009
— an injury, a death — and the paper routing that dispute to an arbitrator was

145
00:07:54,009 --> 00:07:55,960
signed before the injury existed.

146
00:07:56,260 --> 00:07:59,929
From that the court drew the public policy that gave the case its shape.

147
00:08:00,229 --> 00:08:03,340
Its own sentence, quoted back to it the next year in Washington.

148
00:08:03,640 --> 00:08:08,297
As a matter of public policy under West Virginia law, an arbitration clause in a

149
00:08:08,297 --> 00:08:13,070
nursing home admission agreement adopted prior to an occurrence of negligence that

150
00:08:13,070 --> 00:08:17,669
results in a personal injury or wrongful death, shall not be enforced to compel

151
00:08:17,669 --> 00:08:20,637
arbitration of a dispute concerning the negligence.

152
00:08:20,937 --> 00:08:22,144
Not this clause.

153
00:08:22,444 --> 00:08:24,162
Not these three clauses.

154
00:08:24,462 --> 00:08:28,502
Any such clause, in any nursing home admission agreement in the state.

155
00:08:28,802 --> 00:08:31,681
The third holding was about these particular papers.

156
00:08:31,981 --> 00:08:36,874
In two of the three cases, the court ruled that the arbitration clauses were

157
00:08:36,874 --> 00:08:40,480
unconscionable and unenforceable — Brown's and Taylor's.

158
00:08:40,780 --> 00:08:45,018
In the third, Marchio's, it answered the certified question and held that the

159
00:08:45,018 --> 00:08:49,311
Nursing Home Act could not be relied upon to bar enforcement of an arbitration

160
00:08:49,311 --> 00:08:51,183
clause in a nursing home contract.

161
00:08:51,483 --> 00:08:53,619
So the court had built two separate doors.

162
00:08:53,919 --> 00:08:57,402
One was a public policy against a whole category of agreement.

163
00:08:57,702 --> 00:09:02,578
The other was the ordinary law of contracts, applied to specific pieces of paper.

164
00:09:02,878 --> 00:09:05,572
Brown One also said something about admission day.

165
00:09:05,872 --> 00:09:10,795
The process of signing paperwork for medical care — specifically, a contract for

166
00:09:10,795 --> 00:09:15,903
admission to a nursing home — is often fraught with urgency, confusion, and stress.

167
00:09:16,203 --> 00:09:20,692
Most patients, it wrote, do not view the admission process as an interstate

168
00:09:20,692 --> 00:09:24,284
commercial transaction with far-reaching legal consequences.

169
00:09:24,584 --> 00:09:29,318
A year later, in the opinion it wrote after Washington sent the cases back, it added

170
00:09:29,318 --> 00:09:30,389
two more sentences.

171
00:09:30,689 --> 00:09:35,305
Nursing homes daily sign contracts with patients as a routine course of doing

172
00:09:35,305 --> 00:09:35,844
business.

173
00:09:36,144 --> 00:09:40,749
People seek medical care in a nursing home for long-term treatment to heal, and do

174
00:09:40,749 --> 00:09:42,321
so only a few times in life.

175
00:09:42,621 --> 00:09:46,615
The doctrine underneath all that is old, and narrower than it sounds.

176
00:09:46,915 --> 00:09:51,579
Because of an overall and gross imbalance, one-sidedness or lop-sidedness in a

177
00:09:51,579 --> 00:09:56,482
contract, a court may be justified in refusing to enforce the contract as written.

178
00:09:56,782 --> 00:09:59,986
That is unconscionability — a rule about contracts.

179
00:10:00,286 --> 00:10:05,647
In Brown's paper and Taylor's, whoever filed paid to file, and the opinion names no

180
00:10:05,647 --> 00:10:05,905
sum.

181
00:10:06,205 --> 00:10:08,852
The court was careful about what it was not saying.

182
00:10:09,152 --> 00:10:14,278
The courts of this State, it wrote, are not hostile to arbitration or to adhesion

183
00:10:14,278 --> 00:10:14,911
contracts.

184
00:10:15,211 --> 00:10:20,027
We are hostile toward contracts of adhesion that are unconscionable and rely upon

185
00:10:20,027 --> 00:10:24,903
arbitration as an artifice to defraud a weaker party of rights clearly provided by

186
00:10:24,903 --> 00:10:26,449
the common law or statute.

187
00:10:26,749 --> 00:10:28,235
The word is adhesion.

188
00:10:28,535 --> 00:10:33,625
A contract of adhesion is one drafted and imposed by a party of superior strength

189
00:10:33,625 --> 00:10:38,841
that leaves the subscribing party little or no opportunity to alter the substantive

190
00:10:38,841 --> 00:10:43,303
terms, and only the opportunity to adhere to the contract or reject it.

191
00:10:43,603 --> 00:10:45,879
Two of these three forms said the same thing.

192
00:10:46,179 --> 00:10:48,315
The third made no exceptions at all.

193
00:10:48,615 --> 00:10:49,962
Take it or leave it.

194
00:10:50,262 --> 00:10:54,561
The state court's finding, in Brown One: people being admitted to long-term care

195
00:10:54,561 --> 00:10:58,753
facilities and their families have to sign admission contracts without time to

196
00:10:58,753 --> 00:11:02,569
comparison shop or to negotiate the best service and price combination.

197
00:11:02,869 --> 00:11:05,655
Is a take-it-or-leave-it form enough on its own?

198
00:11:05,955 --> 00:11:08,881
Being a form is not a verdict, and the court said so.

199
00:11:09,181 --> 00:11:13,870
Finding that there is an adhesion contract is the beginning point for analysis, not

200
00:11:13,870 --> 00:11:14,661
the end of it.

201
00:11:14,961 --> 00:11:19,381
What courts aim at doing is distinguishing good adhesion contracts which should be

202
00:11:19,381 --> 00:11:22,345
enforced, from bad adhesion contracts which should not.

203
00:11:22,645 --> 00:11:25,989
And then the sentence that gives the whole opinion its edge.

204
00:11:26,289 --> 00:11:31,012
It may be disingenuous for a nursing home to later assert that the patient or family

205
00:11:31,012 --> 00:11:35,735
member consciously, knowingly and deliberately accepted an arbitration clause in the

206
00:11:35,735 --> 00:11:40,289
contract, and understood the clause was intended to eliminate their access to the

207
00:11:40,289 --> 00:11:44,168
courts if the nursing home negligently injured or killed the patient.

208
00:11:44,468 --> 00:11:45,675
Disingenuous.

209
00:11:45,975 --> 00:11:50,015
The defendant nursing homes in all three cases sought review in Washington.

210
00:11:50,315 --> 00:11:54,913
Two petitions for certiorari, docketed as 11-391 and 11-394.

211
00:11:55,213 --> 00:11:58,650
And the defendants specifically challenged Syllabus Point 21.

212
00:11:58,950 --> 00:12:01,086
Not the unconscionability holding.

213
00:12:01,386 --> 00:12:04,358
Not the ruling on the statute, which had gone their way.

214
00:12:04,658 --> 00:12:05,819
The timing rule.

215
00:12:07,619 --> 00:12:10,452
West Virginia had described Washington first.

216
00:12:10,752 --> 00:12:15,375
The state court had to decide whether its public policy was preempted, and to do

217
00:12:15,375 --> 00:12:18,322
that it read the Supreme Court's arbitration cases.

218
00:12:18,622 --> 00:12:22,887
It found them unpersuasive, in language appellate judges do not use about the court

219
00:12:22,887 --> 00:12:23,452
above them.

220
00:12:23,752 --> 00:12:27,839
It called this Court's interpretation of the Federal Arbitration Act tendentious.

221
00:12:28,139 --> 00:12:30,554
It called it created from whole cloth.

222
00:12:30,854 --> 00:12:34,105
The sentence behind the first of those words is in Brown One.

223
00:12:34,405 --> 00:12:38,872
With tendentious reasoning, the United States Supreme Court has stretched the

224
00:12:38,872 --> 00:12:43,571
application of the FAA from being a procedural statutory scheme effective only in

225
00:12:43,571 --> 00:12:48,328
the federal courts, to being a substantive law that preempts state law in both the

226
00:12:48,328 --> 00:12:49,777
federal and state courts.

227
00:12:50,077 --> 00:12:54,349
Underneath the insult was a theory of the statute, and the state court laid it out.

228
00:12:54,649 --> 00:12:59,700
The text of the Federal Arbitration Act, and the congressional history at the time

229
00:12:59,700 --> 00:13:04,443
of its adoption in 1925, suggested to it that the Act was intended to reverse

230
00:13:04,443 --> 00:13:06,538
judicial hostility to arbitration.

231
00:13:06,838 --> 00:13:11,378
It was designed, the court said, to allow commercial entities engaged in interstate

232
00:13:11,378 --> 00:13:15,754
commerce to enter into arbitration agreements that would be enforced nationwide.

233
00:13:16,054 --> 00:13:17,354
Commercial entities.

234
00:13:17,654 --> 00:13:19,001
Nationwide enforcement.

235
00:13:19,301 --> 00:13:22,738
Then it read the recent decisions and said what it thought they were doing.

236
00:13:23,038 --> 00:13:28,040
Much of the reasoning was tendentious, and intended to promote a particular point of

237
00:13:28,040 --> 00:13:32,684
view — namely that arbitration is a panacea for all the woes of litigation and

238
00:13:32,684 --> 00:13:35,066
should therefore be given special favor.

239
00:13:35,366 --> 00:13:36,527
A panacea.

240
00:13:36,827 --> 00:13:41,298
And from that reading, the state court drew the finding it would be reversed for:

241
00:13:41,298 --> 00:13:45,934
that the Supreme Court had never extended the Act to arbitration agreements like the

242
00:13:45,934 --> 00:13:47,369
ones in these three cases.

243
00:13:47,669 --> 00:13:49,387
Then the conclusion it reached.

244
00:13:49,687 --> 00:13:54,470
Congress did not intend for the FAA to be, in any way, applicable to personal injury

245
00:13:54,470 --> 00:13:59,196
or wrongful death suits that only collaterally derive from a written agreement that

246
00:13:59,196 --> 00:14:02,272
evidences a transaction affecting interstate commerce.

247
00:14:02,572 --> 00:14:04,012
Collaterally derive.

248
00:14:04,312 --> 00:14:07,238
Is a negligence claim really a claim on the contract?

249
00:14:07,538 --> 00:14:11,021
It arrives later, out of an event nobody contracted about.

250
00:14:11,321 --> 00:14:16,337
The Supreme Court granted certiorari and decided both cases on February 21, 2012.

251
00:14:16,637 --> 00:14:18,262
Five pages, per curiam.

252
00:14:18,562 --> 00:14:21,302
It opened with the rule and left the courtesies out.

253
00:14:21,602 --> 00:14:26,190
State and federal courts must enforce the Federal Arbitration Act with respect to

254
00:14:26,190 --> 00:14:29,079
all arbitration agreements covered by that statute.

255
00:14:29,379 --> 00:14:34,356
Here, the Supreme Court of Appeals of West Virginia, by misreading and disregarding

256
00:14:34,356 --> 00:14:38,973
the precedents of this Court interpreting the FAA, did not follow controlling

257
00:14:38,973 --> 00:14:41,732
federal law implementing that basic principle.

258
00:14:42,032 --> 00:14:43,797
Misreading and disregarding.

259
00:14:44,097 --> 00:14:47,069
Two words, and they are not the same accusation.

260
00:14:47,369 --> 00:14:48,484
One is error.

261
00:14:48,784 --> 00:14:50,177
The other is choice.

262
00:14:50,477 --> 00:14:54,889
The Court then described what the state court had done, in a single sentence.

263
00:14:55,189 --> 00:14:59,897
The state court held unenforceable all predispute arbitration agreements that apply

264
00:14:59,897 --> 00:15:04,152
to claims alleging personal injury or wrongful death against nursing homes.

265
00:15:04,452 --> 00:15:05,474
All of them.

266
00:15:05,774 --> 00:15:08,142
Next, the statute.

267
00:15:08,442 --> 00:15:14,187
Section 2 of the Federal Arbitration Act, quoted in the opinion: a written provision

268
00:15:14,187 --> 00:15:19,932
in a contract evidencing a transaction involving commerce to settle by arbitration a

269
00:15:19,932 --> 00:15:25,540
controversy thereafter arising out of such contract or transaction shall be valid,

270
00:15:25,540 --> 00:15:31,080
irrevocable, and enforceable, save upon such grounds as exist at law or in equity

271
00:15:31,080 --> 00:15:33,473
for the revocation of any contract.

272
00:15:33,773 --> 00:15:36,002
Valid, irrevocable, and enforceable.

273
00:15:36,302 --> 00:15:41,731
And then, in the same sentence of the statute, the exception that decides everything

274
00:15:41,731 --> 00:15:42,571
that follows.

275
00:15:42,871 --> 00:15:44,171
Keep the second half.

276
00:15:44,471 --> 00:15:45,678
It comes back.

277
00:15:45,978 --> 00:15:48,114
On the first half the Court was brief.

278
00:15:48,414 --> 00:15:52,505
The statute's text includes no exception for personal-injury or wrongful-death

279
00:15:52,505 --> 00:15:52,872
claims.

280
00:15:53,172 --> 00:15:58,524
The Act requires courts to enforce the bargain of the parties to arbitrate — a line

281
00:15:58,524 --> 00:16:02,135
from a 1985 decision, Dean Witter Reynolds against Byrd.

282
00:16:02,435 --> 00:16:07,759
It reflects an emphatic federal policy in favor of arbitral dispute resolution — a

283
00:16:07,759 --> 00:16:12,823
line the Court took from one of its own per curiam opinions of 2011, which was

284
00:16:12,823 --> 00:16:15,160
itself quoting a decision from 1985.

285
00:16:15,460 --> 00:16:18,200
Then the precedent that made the answer mechanical.

286
00:16:18,500 --> 00:16:23,512
When state law prohibits outright the arbitration of a particular type of claim, the

287
00:16:23,512 --> 00:16:27,927
analysis is straightforward: the conflicting rule is displaced by the FAA.

288
00:16:28,227 --> 00:16:32,546
That was AT&T Mobility against Concepcion, decided the previous term.

289
00:16:32,846 --> 00:16:34,796
That rule resolves these cases.

290
00:16:35,096 --> 00:16:39,415
Four words, and then the application, in the Court's own sentence.

291
00:16:39,715 --> 00:16:43,878
West Virginia's prohibition against predispute agreements to arbitrate

292
00:16:43,878 --> 00:16:48,873
personal-injury or wrongful-death claims against nursing homes is a categorical rule

293
00:16:48,873 --> 00:16:53,809
prohibiting arbitration of a particular type of claim, and that rule is contrary to

294
00:16:53,809 --> 00:16:55,830
the terms and coverage of the FAA.

295
00:16:56,130 --> 00:16:58,173
Had a state tried this before?

296
00:16:58,473 --> 00:17:00,981
The Court listed four that had, and lost.

297
00:17:01,281 --> 00:17:06,197
Preston against Ferrer, 2008 — a state commissioner given exclusive jurisdiction

298
00:17:06,197 --> 00:17:09,269
over an issue the parties had agreed to arbitrate.

299
00:17:09,569 --> 00:17:15,877
Mastrobuono, 1995 — a state rule requiring judicial resolution of claims involving

300
00:17:15,877 --> 00:17:17,185
punitive damages.

301
00:17:17,485 --> 00:17:22,231
Perry against Thomas, 1987 — a state requirement that litigants be provided a

302
00:17:22,231 --> 00:17:24,265
judicial forum for wage disputes.

303
00:17:24,565 --> 00:17:29,896
Southland against Keating, 1984 — a state investment statute banning arbitration of

304
00:17:29,896 --> 00:17:31,438
claims brought under it.

305
00:17:31,738 --> 00:17:32,713
Preempted.

306
00:17:33,013 --> 00:17:33,988
Preempted.

307
00:17:34,288 --> 00:17:35,310
Preempted.

308
00:17:35,610 --> 00:17:36,585
Preempted.

309
00:17:36,885 --> 00:17:40,275
To the language about tendentious reasoning, one sentence.

310
00:17:40,575 --> 00:17:44,883
The West Virginia court's interpretation of the FAA was both incorrect and

311
00:17:44,883 --> 00:17:48,841
inconsistent with clear instruction in the precedents of this Court.

312
00:17:49,141 --> 00:17:53,631
Then the constitutional point, which is the shortest passage in the opinion and the

313
00:17:53,631 --> 00:17:54,064
largest.

314
00:17:54,364 --> 00:17:58,735
When this Court has fulfilled its duty to interpret federal law, a state court may

315
00:17:58,735 --> 00:18:01,934
not contradict or fail to implement the rule so established.

316
00:18:02,234 --> 00:18:04,324
The citation is the Supremacy Clause.

317
00:18:04,624 --> 00:18:08,246
The decision of the State Supreme Court of Appeals must be vacated.

318
00:18:10,046 --> 00:18:13,575
To vacate a judgment is to wipe it out and send the case back.

319
00:18:13,875 --> 00:18:16,290
It is not a ruling that the other side wins.

320
00:18:16,590 --> 00:18:20,849
And here the Supreme Court was unusually explicit about how much it was leaving

321
00:18:20,849 --> 00:18:22,952
undone, because it wrote a second part.

322
00:18:23,252 --> 00:18:24,924
Part Two is one page long.

323
00:18:25,224 --> 00:18:28,150
It begins with the holding nobody had appealed.

324
00:18:28,450 --> 00:18:32,658
The West Virginia court proposed an alternative holding that the particular

325
00:18:32,658 --> 00:18:36,809
arbitration clauses in Brown's case and Taylor's case were unconscionable.

326
00:18:37,109 --> 00:18:41,845
As to Marchio, the Supreme Court added a note inside its own sentence: the state

327
00:18:41,845 --> 00:18:46,758
court had not addressed the question whether the arbitration agreement in Marchio's

328
00:18:46,758 --> 00:18:50,251
case is unenforceable for reasons other than public policy.

329
00:18:50,551 --> 00:18:55,976
Two of the three papers had been condemned twice — once by a categorical rule, once

330
00:18:55,976 --> 00:18:57,610
by ordinary contract law.

331
00:18:57,910 --> 00:19:03,236
The third had been condemned once, on the categorical rule alone, and examined on no

332
00:19:03,236 --> 00:19:04,504
other ground at all.

333
00:19:04,804 --> 00:19:08,148
The Court's problem with the alternative holding was contamination.

334
00:19:08,448 --> 00:19:13,136
It is unclear, however, to what degree the state court's alternative holding was

335
00:19:13,136 --> 00:19:17,648
influenced by the invalid, categorical rule discussed above, the rule against

336
00:19:17,648 --> 00:19:19,640
predispute arbitration agreements.

337
00:19:19,940 --> 00:19:21,565
And it produced the example.

338
00:19:21,865 --> 00:19:26,714
In its discussion of the alternative holding, the state court had found the

339
00:19:26,714 --> 00:19:31,563
arbitration clauses unconscionable in part because a predispute arbitration

340
00:19:31,563 --> 00:19:35,054
agreement of this kind clearly violates public policy.

341
00:19:35,354 --> 00:19:40,392
There it was, sitting in the middle of the contract analysis: the rule that had just

342
00:19:40,392 --> 00:19:45,251
been struck out, doing work inside the finding that was supposed to stand without

343
00:19:45,251 --> 00:19:45,431
it.

344
00:19:45,731 --> 00:19:47,217
So, the instruction.

345
00:19:47,517 --> 00:19:52,812
On remand, the West Virginia court must consider whether, absent that general public

346
00:19:52,812 --> 00:19:58,044
policy, the arbitration clauses in Brown's case and Taylor's case are unenforceable

347
00:19:58,044 --> 00:20:02,709
under state common law principles that are not specific to arbitration and

348
00:20:02,709 --> 00:20:04,096
pre-empted by the FAA.

349
00:20:04,396 --> 00:20:07,879
The last clause is the savings clause from section 2, back again.

350
00:20:08,179 --> 00:20:12,606
A state may not write a rule that says: this type of claim does not go to

351
00:20:12,606 --> 00:20:13,334
arbitration.

352
00:20:13,634 --> 00:20:17,256
That is what West Virginia had written, and that is what fell.

353
00:20:17,556 --> 00:20:22,510
A state may say: this contract is unconscionable, and we would say so of any

354
00:20:22,510 --> 00:20:27,855
contract with these features, whether or not the word arbitration ever appeared in

355
00:20:27,855 --> 00:20:28,051
it.

356
00:20:28,351 --> 00:20:31,834
That is exactly the ground section 2 preserves, in terms.

357
00:20:32,134 --> 00:20:33,574
Then the disposition.

358
00:20:33,874 --> 00:20:36,150
The petition for certiorari is granted.

359
00:20:36,450 --> 00:20:40,984
The judgment of the Supreme Court of Appeals of West Virginia is vacated, and the

360
00:20:40,984 --> 00:20:44,902
cases are remanded for proceedings not inconsistent with this opinion.

361
00:20:45,202 --> 00:20:49,342
The Supreme Court did not hold that any of these three arbitration clauses was

362
00:20:49,342 --> 00:20:49,660
valid.

363
00:20:49,960 --> 00:20:52,561
It did not order anyone to arbitrate anything.

364
00:20:52,861 --> 00:20:56,938
It did not decide whether these families could sue, and it did not decide whether

365
00:20:56,938 --> 00:21:00,663
the papers they were holding meant what the nursing homes said they meant.

366
00:21:00,963 --> 00:21:05,363
It held that one rule West Virginia had invented was preempted, and that a judgment

367
00:21:05,363 --> 00:21:07,325
resting on that rule could not stand.

368
00:21:07,625 --> 00:21:09,436
Everything else went back.

369
00:21:09,736 --> 00:21:14,619
It also left untouched a question the record does not answer: whether a relative's

370
00:21:14,619 --> 00:21:19,442
signature on an admission form can bind a patient, or a patient's estate, at all.

371
00:21:19,742 --> 00:21:22,250
That question was raised in the litigation.

372
00:21:22,550 --> 00:21:23,850
It is in the file.

373
00:21:24,150 --> 00:21:26,240
The five pages do not mention it.

374
00:21:26,540 --> 00:21:31,762
And the opinion says nothing whatever about a contract that arbitrates a death claim

375
00:21:31,762 --> 00:21:34,435
while leaving a debt claim in a courthouse.

376
00:21:34,735 --> 00:21:36,732
That clause was in front of the Court.

377
00:21:37,032 --> 00:21:40,097
It is quoted in the Court's own recitation of the facts.

378
00:21:40,397 --> 00:21:42,812
It was not what the defendants had challenged.

379
00:21:43,112 --> 00:21:45,480
This was a per curiam opinion.

380
00:21:45,780 --> 00:21:47,730
Per curiam means by the court.

381
00:21:48,030 --> 00:21:52,535
There is no author, no reported vote, and no separate writing attached to the text.

382
00:21:52,835 --> 00:21:57,880
It is not a synonym for unanimous, and nobody should be told which Justices stood

383
00:21:57,880 --> 00:22:00,683
behind it, because the document does not say.

384
00:22:00,983 --> 00:22:05,394
So the file went back to Charleston with a mandate in it, and an unanswered question

385
00:22:05,394 --> 00:22:06,602
underneath the mandate.

386
00:22:06,902 --> 00:22:09,921
The West Virginia court did not treat that as a formality.

387
00:22:10,221 --> 00:22:15,316
In an order dated April 3, 2012, it directed the parties to submit additional briefs

388
00:22:15,316 --> 00:22:16,165
and arguments.

389
00:22:16,465 --> 00:22:18,090
It heard the cases again.

390
00:22:18,390 --> 00:22:22,714
At that rehearing, counsel for the plaintiffs argued that because the trial courts

391
00:22:22,714 --> 00:22:26,986
had never permitted the parties to develop evidence on unconscionability, parties

392
00:22:26,986 --> 00:22:30,836
challenging an arbitration clause must have a right to conduct discovery.

393
00:22:31,136 --> 00:22:33,876
Counsel for Marmet Health Care Center argued the reverse.

394
00:22:34,176 --> 00:22:38,933
Brown One had been wrong, they said, because there was insufficient evidence in the

395
00:22:38,933 --> 00:22:43,232
record to support a finding that the arbitration clause was unconscionable.

396
00:22:43,532 --> 00:22:47,108
Both sides, in other words, were now arguing about an empty file.

397
00:22:47,408 --> 00:22:50,055
The cases were submitted on June 6, 2012.

398
00:22:50,355 --> 00:22:52,816
The opinion came down seven days later.

399
00:22:54,616 --> 00:22:56,288
June 13, 2012.

400
00:22:56,588 --> 00:23:01,532
Ketchum, Chief Justice, writing for the West Virginia court, in the second opinion —

401
00:23:01,532 --> 00:23:03,415
the one that followed Brown One.

402
00:23:03,715 --> 00:23:06,826
The first move is obedience, and it is exact.

403
00:23:07,126 --> 00:23:11,914
In accordance with the Supreme Court's mandate, we overrule Syllabus Point 21 of

404
00:23:11,914 --> 00:23:12,513
Brown One.

405
00:23:12,813 --> 00:23:13,788
Overrule.

406
00:23:14,088 --> 00:23:16,456
Not vacate, not modify, not distinguish.

407
00:23:16,756 --> 00:23:20,553
The holding that had gone to Washington was taken out of West Virginia law by the

408
00:23:20,553 --> 00:23:21,772
court that had written it.

409
00:23:22,072 --> 00:23:24,301
So had West Virginia simply surrendered?

410
00:23:24,601 --> 00:23:26,784
The next sentence, in the same paragraph:

411
00:23:27,084 --> 00:23:31,442
We otherwise find that the Supreme Court's decision does not counsel us to alter our

412
00:23:31,442 --> 00:23:34,607
original analysis of West Virginia's common law of contracts.

413
00:23:34,907 --> 00:23:39,872
And then, plainly: we otherwise reaffirm all of our discussion and holdings in Brown

414
00:23:39,872 --> 00:23:40,108
One.

415
00:23:40,408 --> 00:23:45,024
And in the next paragraph: however, in light of the parties' additional briefs and

416
00:23:45,024 --> 00:23:47,838
arguments, we modify our conclusions in Brown One.

417
00:23:48,138 --> 00:23:50,506
Both sentences are in the same opinion.

418
00:23:50,806 --> 00:23:53,917
The state court had noticed something about the five pages.

419
00:23:54,217 --> 00:23:59,309
The Supreme Court's opinion did not discuss any other portion of Brown One, aside

420
00:23:59,309 --> 00:24:02,390
from Syllabus Point 21 and its accompanying text.

421
00:24:02,690 --> 00:24:04,966
One holding out of three had been reversed.

422
00:24:05,266 --> 00:24:09,499
The other two were untouched, and the court that wrote them declined to read silence

423
00:24:09,499 --> 00:24:10,003
as defeat.

424
00:24:10,303 --> 00:24:15,322
On the first of them: the parties have not challenged our holding in Syllabus Point

425
00:24:15,322 --> 00:24:20,280
11 of Brown One regarding the preemption of section 15(c) by section 2 of the FAA,

426
00:24:20,280 --> 00:24:21,913
and we need not revisit it.

427
00:24:22,213 --> 00:24:27,485
It then described the doctrine it was keeping, in the exact vocabulary the mandate

428
00:24:27,485 --> 00:24:28,064
had used.

429
00:24:28,364 --> 00:24:33,809
The doctrine of unconscionability that we explicated in Brown One is a general,

430
00:24:33,809 --> 00:24:39,460
state, common-law, contract-law principle that is not specific to arbitration, and

431
00:24:39,460 --> 00:24:41,321
does not implicate the FAA.

432
00:24:41,621 --> 00:24:45,336
And the court did not take back the language that had got it reversed.

433
00:24:45,636 --> 00:24:48,422
Here is how it described what had happened to it.

434
00:24:48,722 --> 00:24:53,868
The Supreme Court — without elucidating how and why the FAA applies to negligence

435
00:24:53,868 --> 00:24:59,014
actions that arise subsequently and only incidentally to a contract containing an

436
00:24:59,014 --> 00:25:04,096
arbitration clause — summarily concluded that the holding was a categorical rule

437
00:25:04,096 --> 00:25:07,019
contrary to the terms and coverage of the FAA.

438
00:25:07,319 --> 00:25:08,341
Summarily.

439
00:25:08,641 --> 00:25:10,824
Without elucidating how and why.

440
00:25:11,124 --> 00:25:15,726
That is a court accepting a result while declining to accept the reasoning, in the

441
00:25:15,726 --> 00:25:17,858
very document it was ordered to write.

442
00:25:18,158 --> 00:25:23,366
What it kept was the body of contract law it had assembled in 2011 — the adhesion

443
00:25:23,366 --> 00:25:26,517
analysis, the sliding scale, the whole apparatus.

444
00:25:26,817 --> 00:25:31,968
On the sliding scale, in the words it reaffirmed: a contract term is unenforceable

445
00:25:31,968 --> 00:25:37,056
if it is both procedurally and substantively unconscionable, but both need not be

446
00:25:37,056 --> 00:25:38,752
present to the same degree.

447
00:25:39,052 --> 00:25:44,094
The more substantively oppressive the contract term, the less evidence of procedural

448
00:25:44,094 --> 00:25:48,536
unconscionability is required to come to the conclusion that the clause is

449
00:25:48,536 --> 00:25:50,337
unenforceable, and vice versa.

450
00:25:50,637 --> 00:25:53,981
On the substance of a term, this is the sentence it kept.

451
00:25:54,281 --> 00:25:58,706
Substantive unconscionability may manifest itself in the form of an agreement

452
00:25:58,706 --> 00:26:02,269
requiring arbitration only for the claims of the weaker party.

453
00:26:02,569 --> 00:26:05,820
But a choice of forums for the claims of the stronger party.

454
00:26:06,120 --> 00:26:10,410
Agreements to arbitrate, the court wrote, must contain at least a modicum of

455
00:26:10,410 --> 00:26:12,668
bilaterality to avoid unconscionability.

456
00:26:13,268 --> 00:26:17,355
The West Virginia court did not apply that doctrine to that clause in this opinion.

457
00:26:17,655 --> 00:26:20,023
It could not, and it said why.

458
00:26:20,323 --> 00:26:23,434
It had a sentence of the Supreme Court's own to lean on.

459
00:26:23,734 --> 00:26:28,273
Claims of coercion, fraud, or unequal bargaining power in the formation of an

460
00:26:28,273 --> 00:26:33,225
arbitration agreement are, that Court has said, best left for resolution in specific

461
00:26:33,225 --> 00:26:33,579
cases.

462
00:26:33,879 --> 00:26:38,358
Given that position, the state court wrote, further development of the factual

463
00:26:38,358 --> 00:26:40,195
record by the parties is proper.

464
00:26:40,495 --> 00:26:43,281
Then its own account of the two records in front of it.

465
00:26:43,581 --> 00:26:48,258
The circuit court's order in Brown's case is devoid of any findings of fact or

466
00:26:48,258 --> 00:26:51,615
conclusions of law on the question of unconscionability.

467
00:26:51,915 --> 00:26:56,513
Taylor's order had some findings of fact, but that court had never comprehensively

468
00:26:56,513 --> 00:27:00,831
analysed the question under the guidelines the state court laid down in 2011.

469
00:27:01,131 --> 00:27:05,450
There was no evidence to weigh, because nobody had been permitted to take any.

470
00:27:05,750 --> 00:27:10,269
Its own line for a record in that condition, from Brown One: without factual or

471
00:27:10,269 --> 00:27:14,388
legal findings, this Court is greatly at sea without a chart or compass.

472
00:27:14,688 --> 00:27:17,985
So it sent both cases down rather than answering them.

473
00:27:18,285 --> 00:27:19,725
In its own words.

474
00:27:20,025 --> 00:27:24,356
We conclude the correct course is to remand these cases to the circuit courts for

475
00:27:24,356 --> 00:27:28,740
the taking of evidence, the full development of a record, and proper consideration

476
00:27:28,740 --> 00:27:30,985
of whether the clauses are unconscionable.

477
00:27:31,285 --> 00:27:36,053
Two further arguments were on the table, and the court refused both — not on their

478
00:27:36,053 --> 00:27:38,205
merits, but on the order of business.

479
00:27:38,505 --> 00:27:42,592
The plaintiffs had argued that the clauses were impossible to comply with.

480
00:27:42,892 --> 00:27:46,769
The Marmet clause required binding arbitration in accordance with the Commercial

481
00:27:46,769 --> 00:27:49,579
Arbitration Rules of the American Arbitration Association.

482
00:27:49,879 --> 00:27:55,039
Effective January 1, 2003, that association had announced it would no longer accept

483
00:27:55,039 --> 00:28:00,013
the administration of cases involving individual patients without a post-dispute

484
00:28:00,013 --> 00:28:01,443
agreement to arbitrate.

485
00:28:01,743 --> 00:28:05,795
The Clarksburg clause required arbitration in accordance with the Code of Procedure

486
00:28:05,795 --> 00:28:07,455
of the National Arbitration Forum.

487
00:28:07,755 --> 00:28:13,356
That forum had announced it would cease to administer consumer arbitration disputes

488
00:28:13,356 --> 00:28:18,822
as of Friday, July 24, 2009, as part of a settlement agreement with the Minnesota

489
00:28:18,822 --> 00:28:19,969
Attorney General.

490
00:28:20,269 --> 00:28:24,358
The forums named in the papers had stopped taking the kind of case the papers were

491
00:28:24,358 --> 00:28:25,006
sending them.

492
00:28:25,306 --> 00:28:28,789
The second argument is the one the Supreme Court never reached.

493
00:28:29,089 --> 00:28:33,750
There is no evidence in the record, the plaintiffs said, that the family member who

494
00:28:33,750 --> 00:28:38,130
signed each admission agreement had the legal authority to sign an arbitration

495
00:28:38,130 --> 00:28:42,679
clause, and thereby waive the resident's or the resident's heirs' right to pursue

496
00:28:42,679 --> 00:28:43,578
action in court.

497
00:28:43,878 --> 00:28:48,132
We decline to consider these two arguments, the court wrote, which should be

498
00:28:48,132 --> 00:28:50,147
considered by the trial court first.

499
00:28:50,447 --> 00:28:53,466
Then the disposition, and it is not one thing.

500
00:28:53,766 --> 00:28:55,531
Brown: reversed and remanded.

501
00:28:55,831 --> 00:28:57,131
Taylor: the same.

502
00:28:57,431 --> 00:28:59,985
Marchio: the certified question answered.

503
00:29:00,285 --> 00:29:05,115
Yes — the Nursing Home Act's waiver ban is preempted by the Federal Arbitration Act.

504
00:29:05,415 --> 00:29:09,747
Unconscionability was left to be raised by the parties on remand, because the trial

505
00:29:09,747 --> 00:29:11,313
court had never considered it.

506
00:29:11,613 --> 00:29:15,746
Nothing in that second opinion holds these clauses unenforceable.

507
00:29:16,046 --> 00:29:18,229
Nothing in it holds them enforceable either.

508
00:29:18,529 --> 00:29:21,501
It hands the question back to the circuit courts and stops.

509
00:29:22,101 --> 00:29:23,355
So what was the paper?

510
00:29:23,655 --> 00:29:26,674
On the record these two opinions make, it was this.

511
00:29:26,974 --> 00:29:31,967
A form signed at an admission desk by a family member, on behalf of a patient who

512
00:29:31,967 --> 00:29:33,940
required extensive nursing care.

513
00:29:34,240 --> 00:29:39,913
A form that sent every dispute to a private arbitrator, except one: the home's own

514
00:29:39,913 --> 00:29:41,020
claim for money.

515
00:29:41,320 --> 00:29:44,339
A form that made whoever filed first pay to file.

516
00:29:44,639 --> 00:29:48,772
And in one of the three cases, a form with no exceptions in it at all.

517
00:29:49,072 --> 00:29:54,472
It was, the Supreme Court held, a written provision in a contract evidencing a

518
00:29:54,472 --> 00:30:00,149
transaction involving commerce — and therefore inside an Act of Congress passed in

519
00:30:00,149 --> 00:30:00,496
1925.

520
00:30:00,796 --> 00:30:03,025
That is what Washington decided.

521
00:30:03,325 --> 00:30:06,204
It decided the paper was covered by the statute.

522
00:30:06,504 --> 00:30:08,780
It did not decide the paper was good.

523
00:30:09,080 --> 00:30:11,681
Section 2 carries both halves in one sentence.

524
00:30:11,981 --> 00:30:16,532
Enforceable — and revocable on any ground that would revoke any other contract.

525
00:30:16,832 --> 00:30:19,340
The savings clause was there the whole time.

526
00:30:19,640 --> 00:30:23,866
What the Supreme Court decided was that West Virginia had reached past it.

527
00:30:24,166 --> 00:30:28,677
West Virginia gave up the rule it had been reversed on and kept everything else,

528
00:30:28,677 --> 00:30:33,414
including the doctrine that asks whether a contract is so one-sided that a court may

529
00:30:33,414 --> 00:30:35,219
refuse to enforce it as written.

530
00:30:35,519 --> 00:30:38,584
Which leaves the story where the documents leave it.

531
00:30:38,884 --> 00:30:40,231
Three families sued.

532
00:30:40,531 --> 00:30:42,017
Three papers were signed.

533
00:30:42,317 --> 00:30:46,699
What the litigation settled was which sovereign's law applied to those papers, and

534
00:30:46,699 --> 00:30:49,051
almost nothing about whether they were fair.

535
00:30:49,351 --> 00:30:52,741
Two questions were still standing when both opinions closed.

536
00:30:53,041 --> 00:30:57,263
Whether a paper that arbitrates a death and litigates a debt is lop-sided enough

537
00:30:57,263 --> 00:31:00,007
that a court should refuse to enforce it as written.

538
00:31:00,307 --> 00:31:04,119
And whether the family member who signed it had the authority to sign away anything

539
00:31:04,119 --> 00:31:04,440
at all.

540
00:31:04,740 --> 00:31:06,365
Neither had been answered.

541
00:31:06,665 --> 00:31:10,705
The first was sent back down to be argued on a record that did not exist yet.

542
00:31:11,005 --> 00:31:13,513
The second the court declined to reach at all.

543
00:31:13,813 --> 00:31:16,785
The clause, once more, as the two papers wrote it.

544
00:31:17,085 --> 00:31:21,270
To arbitrate all disputes, other than claims to collect late payments owed by the

545
00:31:21,270 --> 00:31:21,683
patient.
